  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing YouTube Playback**\n\nI\'m now diving into the specifics of YouTube\'s audio playback and buffering mechanisms. My primary focus is on identifying failure points. Currently, I\'m examining how the `StreamResolver` component extracts the video ID from various URL formats. My attention is on the 11-character video ID, and what happens when it is not possible to extract that ID.\n\n\n**Exploring Audio Playback Options**\n\nI\'ve moved on to examining the practicalities of audio playback. It looks like a reliable solution is achievable by using an Android WebView and the official YouTube IFrame Player. I\'m focusing on building a bridge using the `WebView` that can run the official YouTube embed, handle events, and control playback via JavaScript interfaces. Specifically, the proposed solution uses the format `https://www.youtube.com/embed/<videoId>?enablejsapi=1&autoplay=1` to stream audio via Android\'s audio system.\n\n\n**Implementing Playback Control**\n\nNow I\'m focusing on the UI and control aspects of playback. I\'m nailing down the state transitions: `onPlaying()`, `onPaused()`, and `onTimeUpdate()`. I\'ve outlined how these events will update the UI, specifically the play/pause state, loading status, and scrubber. I\'m also ensuring the `DualDeckAudioEngine` can manage both YouTube and local audio seamlessly, handling transitions between players. The YouTube IFrame Player guarantees robustness!\n\n\n**Verifying Headless Playback**\n\nI\'m now checking the headless capabilities of the `WebView`. I\'m confirming that a `WebView` with JavaScript and media enabled can indeed load an HTML page with the YouTube IFrame API and play audio in the background on Android. I\'ve uncovered some potential caveats: the `WebView` might pause audio when minimized on certain Android versions, particularly if not attached to a window. To circumvent this, I\'m considering using an `AndroidView` within Jetpack Compose, hidden or compact, within the UI!\n\n\n**Refining Stream Resolution**\n\nI\'m now refining the `StreamResolver` to prioritize direct audio stream extraction from Invidious or Cobalt before falling back to the YouTube audio bridge. My goal is to streamline the audio handling process. I\'m focusing on the logic to extract direct audio URLs and have the `MediaPlayer` play them directly. I\'m also ensuring the bridge handles tracks prefixed with \"yt:\", \"https://www.youtube\", or \"https://youtu.be\" by rewriting them to \"youtube:$videoId\" for the audio bridge. The goal is a unified playback solution for all track sources.\n\n\n**Validating UI State**\n\nNow I\'m focusing on the `DeckUiState` in `DualDeckAudioEngine.kt`. I\'m going through its properties to ensure they accurately reflect the current state of playback and are sufficient for UI updates. Currently, my concern revolves around whether they cover all the scenarios, such as loading, buffering, track information, playback status, and volume. I\'ll make sure to add what\'s missing, such as the visualizer state!\n\n\n**Enhancing UI Indicators**\n\nI\'m now integrating new properties into `DeckUiState`, specifically `isBuffering`, `bufferPercent`, and `statusText`, to improve UI feedback during audio streaming. I\'ve designed the UI to display a loading bar and a status message, ensuring users are informed during buffering. The `statusText` will dynamically update to reflect the state of the audio stream, enhancing the user experience, especially during loading or buffering.\n\n\n**Constructing the YouTube Bridge**\n\nI\'m now implementing the `YouTubeAudioBridge.kt` within the engine. My focus is on managing the `WebView` instances, one per deck, to streamline YouTube audio playback. I\'m focusing on the HTML, specifically the integration of the YouTube IFrame API to control audio streams via JavaScript, and its effective integration within our UI.\n\n\n**Integrating YouTube Events**\n\nI\'m now integrating the YouTube IFrame API events within my bridge. I\'m focusing on the `onReady`, `onStateChange`, and `onError` functions. I\'ll pass the `event.data` to the bridge for UI updates, streamlining playback control and error handling. I\'m ensuring `pendingVideoId` and `pendingAutoPlay` are managed correctly for initial playback.\n\n\n**Implementing JavaScript Communication**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the JavaScript code to run within the `WebView` for the YouTube bridge. I\'m building functions like `loadVideo`, `play`, `pause`, `seekTo`, `setVolume`, and `setPlaybackRate` to handle commands. I\'m also implementing the `setInterval` function to periodically call `onProgress`, which sends progress updates from the `WebView` to the app. I\'m ensuring all calls are properly handled for seamless playback control.\n\n\n**Refining Interface Events**\n\nNow, I\'m integrating the JavaScript interface within the `WebView` to streamline YouTube playback events. I\'m focusing on handling events for readiness, state changes, progress updates, and errors via Kotlin. I\'m prioritizing event binding, ensuring robust communication to the Kotlin layer, while also managing the `pendingVideoId` and `pendingAutoPlay` for seamless playback control.\n\n\n**Validating Direct Streams**\n\nI\'m now confirming the dual-deck engine\'s capability with diverse audio streams. I\'m verifying the seamless handling of various formats like MP3, AAC, FLAC, M3U8, and radio streams. I\'m ensuring Android\'s `MediaPlayer` handles these natively while I manage the switching between local and YouTube streams flawlessly. I\'m focusing on validating these with the Visualizer.\n\n\n**Correcting Visualizer Behavior**\n\nI\'ve addressed an issue in `VisualizerEngine` where the visualization continued generating frames even when paused, causing visual artifacts. I\'ve introduced `isPlaying`, `bpm`, `vuLevel`, and `positionMs` and updated the playback state for correct rendering. The render loop now uses `decayToSilence()` when the player is paused, which smoothly decays the visuals to a silent state, improving UI behavior.\n\n\n**Synchronizing Visualizer Behavior**\n\nI\'m now implementing the beat synchronization for the visualizer, ensuring accurate UI feedback. The objective is to make the visualizer react realistically to playback states, including on/off beat transients. I\'ve integrated a new beat-phase calculation that responds to BPM and the current playback position. I\'m focusing on the bass frequencies pulsing on the beat, and the waveform oscillating according to the song\'s transients, ensuring a synchronized, realistic visual output. If hardware visualizer active, the UI strictly respects `if (!isPlaying)`.\n\n\n**Implementing Track Deletion**\n\nI\'m now implementing track deletion within the UI and the data layer, allowing local song removal. The user can now tap a delete button within the library to remove local tracks. I\'ve integrated a confirmation dialog, ensuring that deletion is a deliberate action, while the UI instantly reflects the changes. Additionally, the function also removes the local track if it is currently playing on either deck.\n\n\n**Enhancing URL Parsing**\n\nI\'m now refining the `StreamResolver` in `MainActivity.kt`, focusing on robust YouTube URL extraction, including formats like `youtu.be`, `music.youtube.com`, and `m.youtube.com`, even when mixed with text. The system automatically loads shared URLs to Deck A, adds tracks to the library, and starts them automatically. I\'m adding `insertTrack` in the database so the tracks appears in the library.\n\n\n**Improving UI Buffering**\n\nI\'m now integrating UI updates to visualize buffering states for YouTube Music streams. I\'m focusing on incorporating an animated progress bar and status banner within the `DeckCard` to indicate loading and buffering. The goal is to provide more informative feedback on the audio stream loading process. I\'m making sure it uses dynamic text for the banner, displaying buffer percentages!\n\n\n**Visualizing Buffer Progress**\n\nI\'m now integrating enhanced UI feedback for YouTube stream loading. I\'m focusing on adding an animated progress bar and status text within the `DeckCard` to illustrate buffering. I\'ve designed the UI to dynamically show buffer percentages and a \"Resolving...\" indicator, improving the user\'s experience. I am paying close attention to the `StreamResolver` to ensure accurate and dynamic updates.\n\n\n**Building the YouTube Bridge**\n\nI\'m now diving into the implementation of `YouTubeAudioBridge`. The goal is to make it play `youtube:$videoId` formatted streams correctly within the dual-deck engine. I\'ll design the bridge in `com.example.engine.YouTubeAudioBridge.kt`. It will use an Android `Context`, run on the Main thread, and configure a `WebView` with JavaScript, DOM storage, and a specified user agent. It\'ll also load HTML with the YouTube IFrame API and use callback listeners for `onReady`, `onStateChange`, and `onProgress` events.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
