  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing YouTube\'s Mobile Behavior**\n\nI\'ve been examining the JavaScript code, specifically within the `HTML_TEMPLATE`, to understand how YouTube\'s embedded player functions on mobile devices. I\'ve focused on `loadVideo(videoId, autoPlay)`, noting how it utilizes `player.loadVideoById(videoId)`. The critical observation is that `player.unMute()` isn\'t explicitly called, yet the player often unmutes itself, indicating YouTube\'s default mobile behavior.\n\n\n**Investigating Mobile Autoplay Behavior**\n\nI\'ve confirmed that on mobile WebViews, YouTube defaults players to muted states upon loading via `loadVideoById`.  I\'m now certain `player.unMute()` and `player.setVolume(100)` are critical, and should be in `loadVideo`, `onPlayerReady`, and the `play` function. I\'m also examining `YouTubeAudioBridge.kt`, specifically lines 43-59, where the WebView is initialized to determine if `webView?.onResume()` is critical for enabling background audio.\n\n\n**Refining Autoplay Implementation**\n\nI\'ve determined key `settings` for the WebView are crucial: `mediaPlaybackRequiresUserGesture`, `databaseEnabled`, `allowContentAccess`, `allowFileAccess`, and hardware acceleration. In JavaScript, I\'ve updated the `onPlayerReady` function to include explicit `player.unMute()` and `player.setVolume(100)`. I\'ve added these calls in `loadVideo` and `play` to ensure unmuting and full volume, including both `loadVideoById` and `cueVideoById`. I\'ve added conditional statements to handle errors.\n\n\n**Addressing Audio Playback Failure**\n\nI\'ve been meticulously examining the recent sound issue, and I believe I\'ve pinpointed the root cause. My analysis focuses on why audio wasn\'t present, even after the video loaded. I noticed the key is ensuring that `player.unMute()` and `player.setVolume(100)` are always called, and I have added these calls to the critical areas. This includes the `playVideo()` function with a `try...catch` block. I\'ve also incorporated these changes into `setVolume(vol)` function. I am also checking if the WebView needs `onResume()` and `resumeTimers()`! Additionally, I am investigating how WebViews are attached to the Window in Compose, even if hidden.\n\n\n**Confirming Audio Routing**\n\nI\'m now focusing on how Android routes WebView audio directly to the OS mixer, bypassing the application\'s sound controls. Certain OEM versions detach or mute audio from unattached WebViews. To ensure the WebView remains connected, I\'m exploring attachment strategies: using `DualDeckAudioEngine` or `YouTubeAudioBridge` to guarantee window focus. This should fix the issue for sure. I also need to verify the user\'s report regarding YouTube vs local audio!\n\n\n**Debugging Timeline Inconsistency**\n\nI\'m now diving into the timeline\'s erratic behavior during YouTube playback. I\'m focusing on the interaction between the JavaScript `setInterval` function within `YouTubeAudioBridge` and the Kotlin `onProgress` function in `BridgeInterface`. I\'m closely scrutinizing the data flow, specifically the `currentSec`, `totalSec`, and `loadedFraction` values, to ensure accurate conversions to milliseconds. I must check their values and casting! I\'m also examining the `DualDeckAudioEngine`\'s `updateDeck` function to ensure accurate updates. I need to make sure the time values are always valid and correctly handled.\n\n\n**Validating Timeline Updates**\n\nI\'m now investigating potential discrepancies in playback progress within the `DualDeckAudioEngine`. I\'m checking the data flow to the UI, particularly how milliseconds are converted to slider positions. I\'m focusing on the `updatePlaybackPositionsAndVu` function within `DualDeckAudioEngine.kt` and its interaction with the `Slider` in `DualDecksScreen.kt`. I\'m ensuring consistency in `currentPositionMs` and `durationMs` calculations to provide reliable updates.\n\n\n**Isolating Timeline Scrubbing Behavior**\n\nI\'m now completely isolating the slider\'s timeline interactions with a new `TimelineScrubber` composable to fix the thumb jumping issue. The issue stems from the player updates overriding user input during seeking, and the UI reacting too frequently. I will address this via a complete rewrite for clean seeking.\n\n\n**Refining Scrubber Implementation**\n\nI\'m now implementing the `TimelineScrubber` composable, with `isDragging` and `dragProgress` states for smooth seeking. I\'m focusing on consistent `displayProgress` and `displayPositionMs` calculations. I will ensure accurate time display with correct formatting, using `formatMs`, avoiding thumb jump!\n\n\n**Implementing Audio Choice**\n\nI am now focusing on enabling the user to select between two audio streams within the `DualDeckAudioEngine`. I\'m integrating logic to switch between these streams and ensure smooth playback transitions. I will also incorporate an indicator to display the active stream.\n\n\n**Defining UI Behavior**\n\nI\'m now determining how the UI should adapt between single-deck and dual-deck modes. I\'m prioritizing a clean single-deck view as the default, emphasizing a gorgeous audiophile experience. In single-deck mode, I\'ll ensure easy toggling between decks, a full-width turntable with visuals, and large, informative track details. I must ensure the UI is clean.\n\n\n**Designing App Exit Functionality**\n\nI\'m now implementing the exit functionality, focusing on proper resource release. I\'ve designed an exit button in the top app bar with a confirmation dialog. I\'m building out `viewModel.exitApp(context)` to stop playback, release resources, and kill the background service. I will make sure everything is released, and all playback stops.\n\n\n"
